{ignore}
<p><code>{pattern}</code> draws a background or a piece of generative art as SVG on the server:
regular tiles - stripes, dots, a grid, checks, chevrons, isometric cubes, herringbone parquet, brick walls - and pictures the seed
makes up - contour lines of an imagined landscape traced by marching squares, Truchet tiles, a
Voronoi diagram, packed circles, hexagons and triangles in the seed's colours, blobs, film grain
and a soft mesh gradient. Every pattern is a file of <code>pad/subs/patterns/types/</code>; the
files there are the patterns there are.</p>

<p>The same options give the same bytes on every request and every machine: the random numbers
come from PAD's own seeded sequence (the one of <code>padFake</code>, put back afterwards) and
every number is written to a fixed precision. Without <code>seed=</code> the seed is 1, so a
pattern never changes unless it is told to.</p>

<p>A pattern is decoration: <code>aria-hidden</code>, unless <code>title=</code> names it. With
<code>as='url'</code> it is a CSS <code>url()</code> of a data URI, its quotes and the characters a
URL or HTML would read escaped, for a <code>style</code> attribute - a background that repeats at
the width and height given. In a page's PHP, <code>padPattern ( 'topography', [ 'seed' =&gt; 7 ]
)</code> draws the same.</p>

<p>An unknown pattern, a colour that is none, a number outside its bounds and an option the kind
does not take are errors under the strict check; the bare name of a pattern is no tag, as its
names - dots, grid, waves - are those of fields and pages.</p>
{/ignore}
